<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis IV of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 920–<a href="/years/954">954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Louis d'Outremer, Louis IV of West Francia, Louis from Overseas · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis IV, called d'Outremer (&quot;from overseas&quot;), was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from 936 to 954. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a>, he grew up in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was recalled to the throne with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. His reign was marked by struggles with Hugh and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, and his weak royal power foreshadowed the end of Carolingian rule in 987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis IV of France" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis IV (0921-0954; roi des Francs). Autorité émettrice de monnaie Chinon (France; atelier monétaire). Atelier monétaire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Tr%C3%A9sor_de_F%C3%A9camp,_Louis_IV_(936-954),_denier,_Chinon_-_btv1b11306996m_(transparent_version).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was the only son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> and his second wife Eadgifu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>, and so descended from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. After Charles was dethroned and captured in 923, Eadgifu took the infant Louis to Wessex, which gave him the nickname d'Outremer, and he was raised at the courts of Edward and then of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>. He became heir to the western Carolingian line when his father died in captivity in 929. In 936, at fifteen, he was recalled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, to succeed the Robertian king Rudolph. Hugh's envoys invited him to come and take the head of the kingdom, and he received homage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Archbishop Artald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> crowned Louis on 19 June 936, probably at Laon. He spoke neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> and knew little of his kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, who had ruled in fact since 923, became his guardian, and Louis held few lands around the old Carolingian domains and some abbeys. He repeatedly confirmed Hugh's title of duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, in 936, 943 and 954. In 939 Gilbert of Lotharingia rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and offered Louis the crown. Louis married Gilbert's widow Gerberga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> without the consent of her brother Otto, who then invaded Lotharingia.</p><p>In 940 East Frankish forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and expelled Artald. After the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a> in 942 and Herbert II of Vermandois in 943, Louis entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and took the young Richard I into his keeping, and divided the lands of Herbert's sons. He was imprisoned in 945–946. Otto I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy">Conrad I of Burgundy</a> invaded in 946, and the Synod of Ingelheim in June 948 sided with Louis and threatened Hugh with excommunication. In 949 Louis took Laon and in 950 he and Hugh reconciled. He held real authority only north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late summer 954 Louis fell from his horse while trying to capture a wolf in the forest of Voas, on the road from Laon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, and died of his injuries on 10 September. The chronicler Flodoard reports a fall and tuberculosis, while the canons of Reims read the wolf as a divine punishment for his seizure of Corbeny. He was buried at the Abbey of Saint-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remi">Remi</a>, where his widow Gerberga promoted the dynasty's memory. His son Lothair was crowned on 12 November 954 with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, and the regency passed to Hugh and then to Gerberga's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great">Bruno the Great</a>, which marked Ottonian influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis IV of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conrad the Peaceful, who reigned from 937 to 993, owed his throne to Otto I, married Matilda, a daughter of the Carolingian King <b>Louis IV of France</b>, and kept the peace largely through cooperation with bishops and great lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 936 Hugh the Great helped restore the Carolingian <b>Louis IV</b> and was recognised as 'duke of the Franks', second only to the king.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-franks">Duchy of the Franks</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.56,3.62&amp;year=942" aria-label="Laon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4633 437 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis IV of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5083 707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5083 707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.56,3.62&amp;year=942">What was here in 942 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
